# ── App ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
API_URL=http://localhost:4000
API_PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=development

# ── Auth ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JWT_SECRET=change-me-64-random-chars
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=change-me-too-64-random-chars
MAGIC_LINK_TTL_MIN=15
# Session lifetimes. Both have working defaults; set them only to change one.
# The access token is short by design — the refresh cookie is what carries a
# session, and rotation is compare-and-set on the presented hash, so a stolen
# access token expires long before a stolen session can be replayed.
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SEC=900
REFRESH_TTL_DAYS=30

# Magic-link delivery. Three transports, tried in this order:
#   1. Cloudflare Email Service REST API — needs BOTH CF_EMAIL_ACCOUNT_ID and
#      CF_EMAIL_API_TOKEN (Workers Paid plan). Either one alone is ignored.
#   2. SMTP — used when SMTP_HOST is set.
#   3. Neither set: the link is printed to the API log and surfaced in the dev
#      UI. This is the local development sign-in flow.
# A CONFIGURED transport that fails makes POST /auth/magic-link answer 500 —
# never a silent "check your email" for a mail that never went out.
#
# CF_EMAIL_API_TOKEN and SMTP_PASS are SECRETS: set them from your platform's
# secret store, never commit a real value.
CF_EMAIL_ACCOUNT_ID=
CF_EMAIL_API_TOKEN=
CF_EMAIL_FROM=Gather <no-reply@gather.local>
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=Gather <no-reply@gather.local>

# ── Data stores (empty ⇒ in-memory adapters; fine for dev, not prod) ──
MONGO_URL=
REDIS_URL=

# ── Object storage (MinIO / any S3) ───────────────────────────────────
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_ACCESS_KEY=gather
S3_SECRET_KEY=gather-secret
S3_BUCKET=gather-media
S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:9000/gather-media
#
# The five keys above are the WHOLE storage surface the API parses
# (services/api/src/config.ts) — but only the first FOUR are read. Every
# object the API hands out is a freshly presigned URL against S3_ENDPOINT
# (services/api/src/modules/chat/routes.ts redirects /assets/:id/content to
# one), so S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL reaches no code path; attachments.test.ts pins
# that a completed upload's URL does NOT contain it. Region and addressing
# style are NOT env keys:
# both are derived from S3_ENDPOINT when a URL is signed
# (services/api/src/modules/chat/attachments.ts — s3Region / path-style pick
# 'auto' + virtual-hosted for R2/Tigris-shaped hosts, 'us-east-1' + path-style
# otherwise). Setting S3_REGION or S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE does nothing.
#
# A Railway bucket being PRIVATE by default costs nothing here: the presigned
# GET redirect works against a private bucket, which is why no public base is
# needed. A linked Railway bucket's own injected vars (BUCKET /
# ACCESS_KEY_ID / …) are NOT read as an alternative source; set the S3_* keys
# above explicitly.
#
# There is no partial-config boot check for these: leave them unset and the
# localhost MinIO defaults apply, in production too. Only JWT_SECRET and
# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET are enforced at boot.

# FFMPEG_PATH and STORAGE_QUOTA_GB used to live here. They are GONE from
# AppConfig now: services/media took the transcode path with it, chat
# attachments store bytes as-is (hlsUrl is always null), and nothing read
# either value. loadConfig only reads the keys it names, so a deploy that
# still sets them is not an error — the values are simply ignored.

# ── Cloudflare Realtime (TURN fallback + SFU) ─────────────────────────
# CF_TURN_KEY_ID / CF_TURN_API_TOKEN are live: the rtc module mints TURN
# credentials from them (services/api/src/modules/rtc).
CF_TURN_KEY_ID=
CF_TURN_API_TOKEN=
# CF_SFU_APP_ID / CF_SFU_API_TOKEN are parsed into AppConfig
# (services/api/src/config.ts) and read by NOTHING else — the room is a mesh
# and no SFU is dialled today. Kept as the reserved names for the deferred
# server ceiling (docs/CAST_RELAY.md); both default to null when unset.
CF_SFU_APP_ID=
CF_SFU_API_TOKEN=

# (ENABLE_MEDIA_PIPELINE is gone — the key was deleted from config on
# 2026-08-20 and setting it now does nothing; config-media-pipeline.test.ts
# pins the absence so it cannot come back silently ignored.)

# ── Owner ops console (/admin) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated verified account emails that may read ops telemetry and
# resolve abuse reports. Empty = the /admin surface is closed to everyone.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# ── Integrations (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
TENOR_API_KEY=

# ── Web push (generate a key pair with: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys) ─
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@gather.local

# ── Rate limiting ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two tiers, both per-IP: RATE_LIMIT_MAX/WINDOW_MS is the general ceiling and
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX the tighter one on the auth routes — it is the only thing
# bounding how often a stranger's address can be magic-linked.
#
# THE LIMITER IS IN-MEMORY PER INSTANCE. Two API instances mean two independent
# counters, so the real ceiling is max × instances, and a deploy resets both.
# Moving it onto the Redis that is already a hard dependency is tracked in
# docs/FEATURE_PLAN.md item 1.5.
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=20
